.TH LFS-POOL-UNPIN 1 2026-09-14 "Lustre" "Lustre User Utilities"
.SH NAME
lfs-pool-unpin \- unpin files, directories or FIDs from an OST pool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY "lfs pool unpin"
.RB [ --pool
.IR POOL ]
.RB [ --quiet ]
.RB [ --file ]
.IR FILE " | " DIRECTORY " ..."
.SY "lfs pool unpin"
.RB [ --pool
.IR POOL ]
.RB [ --quiet ]
.B --fid
.IR MOUNT
.IR FID " ..."
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B lfs pool unpin
subcommand removes the restriction on the named
.IR FILE (s),
and/or files in the named
.IR DIRECTORY (/ies),
so that there are no longer restrictions on migrating the file from the named
.IR POOL .
.PP
.B lfs pool unpin
removes a
.B pool
entry for the selected
.IR POOL
from the
.B lustre.pin
attribute.  If no matching entry is present, the command succeeds without
modifying the attribute.  When the last entry is removed, the
.B lustre.pin
attribute itself may be deleted.
.PP
When the
.B --pool
option is omitted,
.IR POOL
is inferred from the primary mirror layout of each file or directory.  If the
layout does not have an associated pool, the operation fails for that target
and an error message is printed.
.PP
The
.B --fid
form of the command operates on files referenced by Lustre FIDs beneath the
filesystem mounted at
.IR MOUNT ,
while the default and
.B --file
forms operate on filesystem paths.
.PP
Updating the
.B lustre.pin
attribute requires both appropriate write permissions on the target and
sufficient privileges on the MDT.  If these checks fail the operation will
return
.BR EPERM ,
typically due to the
.B mdt.enable_pin_gid
policy; see
.BR mdt.enable_pin_gid (4)
for details.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --pool " " POOL
Name of the OST pool that should be unpinned from the specified files.  The
name must consist of only alphanumeric characters, underscores and hyphens,
and may optionally be qualified with an
.IR fsname
prefix (e.g.
.IR fsname . POOL ),
which is discarded before the pool name is used.
.TP
.BR -f ", " --file
Interpret remaining arguments as filesystem paths (this is the default if
neither
.B --file
nor
.B --fid
is given).
.TP
.BR -F ", " --fid " " MOUNT
Interpret remaining arguments as Lustre FIDs within the filesystem mounted at
.IR MOUNT .
This option is mutually exclusive with
.BR --file .
.TP
.BR -q ", " --quiet
Suppress per-target error messages (such as a target that cannot be opened or
an unrecognized FID); the exit status still reflects any errors encountered.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Display brief usage information for the
.B lfs pool unpin
subcommand.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The
.B lfs pool unpin
command returns 0 if every target was pinned to the requested (or inferred)
pool and is now unpinned, or 1 if one or more targets were not pinned there to
begin with, could not be unpinned (for example due to insufficient
privileges; see
.BR mdt.enable_pin_gid (4)
for the MDT-side policy controlling which users may pin or unpin files), or
otherwise did not end up unpinned; unpinning a target that had nothing to
unpin is not treated as an error and does not modify
.BR lustre.pin .
A non-zero exit status other than 1 is returned if any argument is invalid,
a pool cannot be inferred when required, or a target could not be opened.
.SH EXAMPLES
Unpin a directory tree and a single file from the pool scratch:
.EX
.B $ lfs pool unpin --pool scratch /mnt/testfs/proj /mnt/testfs/file
.EE
.PP
Unpin a file from the pool inferred from its primary mirror layout:
.EX
.B $ lfs pool unpin /mnt/testfs/file
.EE
.PP
Unpin a file referenced by FID from the pool scratch:
.EX
.B $ lfs pool unpin --pool scratch --fid /mnt/testfs 0x200000401:0x1:0x0
.EE
.SH AVAILABILITY
The
.B lfs pool unpin
command is part of the
.BR lustre (7)
filesystem package since release 2.18.0.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lfs (1),
.BR lfs-pool (1),
.BR lfs-pool-pin (1),
.BR lfs-pool-pin_info (1),
.BR llapi_pool_unpin_file (3),
.BR mdt.enable_pin_gid (4),
.BR lustre (7)
